<TopicPage slug="subjects/political-science/intermediate/the-public-policy-cycle">
  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    The policy cycle is <strong>government's product development loop</strong>: agenda →
    formulation → adoption → implementation → evaluation. The textbook draws it as a circle; the
    reality is messier — windows open rarely, implementation rewrites intent, and evaluation
    rarely kills its targets. Studying the cycle is studying where things actually go wrong.
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="the five stages and their known failure modes" widget="equationStep" config={policyCfg} />

  <h2>What policy analysis actually does</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Market failure or government failure?</strong> The twin lenses (the economics lesson's market failure, plus public-choice scepticism about government). The mature answer is comparative: which institution fails less for THIS problem.</li>
    <li><strong>Evidence-based policy:</strong> trials, pilot programmes, "what works" clearinghouses. The evidence movement changed practice (medicine first — the clinical-trials lesson — now education and crime).</li>
    <li><strong>Policy diffusion:</strong> ideas travel between countries ("policy transfer") — some spread because they work, others because they are fashionable (the sociology of policy fads).</li>
    <li><strong>The multiple-streams caveat:</strong> policies often post-date solutions — a think tank had the design ready, waiting for a crisis to make it thinkable. Look for the waiting solution behind every "sudden" reform.</li>
  </ul>
  <p class="sources"><strong>Sources:</strong> Kingdon, Agendas, Alternatives, and Public Policies (1984); Lipsky, Street-Level Bureaucracy (1980); Howlett & Ramesh, Studying Public Policy.</p>
</TopicPage>
